
export anchor HideBothSidebarsWhilePlanningIsShowing2:
    userPrompt:
        \\\
        While the body's Planning tab is selected, neither sidebar shows. Planning then
        fills the whole width of the window beneath the ribbon, from the window's left edge
        to its right, and from the tab bar down to the bottom.

        The left sidebar is hidden whole, view bar and all. This is an exception to
        spec/ui/components/left-sidebar/index.pi#LeftSidebar.description and
        spec/ui/components/left-sidebar/index.pi#LeftSidebar.viewBar ("The bar is always
        there"). The right sidebar doesn't show either, even while a task runs, the queue
        holds a prompt, or it was opened by hand. This is an exception to
        spec/ui/components/right-sidebar/index.pi#RightSidebar.showing, and the body's
        right-sidebar button and Ctrl+Alt+B (Cmd+Alt+B on macOS) do nothing while Planning
        is selected.

        This works like the chat input, which already hides while Planning is selected, as
        spec/scope/application/Body.pi#Body.tabs says: both sidebars go at once with nothing
        sliding, and come back at once when Chat, a file or a task tab is selected. They
        come back as they were left: the left sidebar's view, collapsed state, width,
        scroll and selection, and the right sidebar's width and whether it was opened by
        hand. If a task started or ended while Planning was showing, the right sidebar comes
        back in whatever state that leaves it.

        Update spec/scope/application/Body.pi#Body.description and
        spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.description so Planning is placed across the
        full window width.
        \\\

    mode: code

    newConversation: true

    systemPrompt:
        \\\
        We're working on the code, located in ./src. Change the code so it does what the prompt asks, as the spec describes it.

        When the work is about Suspense, its modes, its prompts, or its questions, or you are to write a prompt or a question for the user, first read .suspense/suspense-fluency.md once in this conversation, unless you already have: it is how Suspense works.

        The spec's Piton source is in ./spec. Don't change it, and don't read it to learn what the spec says: read the compiled reference instead, as above. If the prompt asks for something the spec doesn't describe, or describes differently, say so in your reply rather than working around it.

        Once you have read what the change depends on, and before your first change, write the constraints this task must meet to ${UNDERSTANDING_FILE}, once. Rewrite it only when a constraint in it turns out to be wrong, never after each page you read. It is a Markdown list and nothing else: one line per constraint, at most twelve, each a single short, specific statement of what must be true, written as a link to the compiled reference file it comes from, such as `- [The chain tab has no bottom border in combined mode](.claude/reference/scope/prompt-mode/chat-input/index.md#chain)`. Only list what the spec says; leave out anything you are deciding yourself. Replace a constraint when you learn it was wrong rather than adding another.

        End with a reply of a few sentences saying what changed, or for a question, the answer, not a recap of the work or of what you read.

        This prompt is the second step of a chain. Its first step changed the spec to meet it, and the spec has been built since. Now change the code so it does what the spec now says, without changing the spec. Follow the spec the prompt references, and the anchors the spec step names below. Where the spec and the prompt disagree, follow the spec, and say so in your reply.

        The code is located in ./src.

        The spec's source, which this step doesn't change, is in ./spec.

        The prompt the chain was sent:

        ${SPEC_PROMPT}

        What the spec step said it changed, its final output:

        ${SPEC_RESULT}
        \\\

    codeTask:
        prompt:
            \\\
            While the body's Planning tab is selected, neither sidebar shows. Planning then
            fills the whole width of the window beneath the ribbon, from the window's left edge
            to its right, and from the tab bar down to the bottom.

            The left sidebar is hidden whole, view bar and all. This is an exception to
            spec/ui/components/left-sidebar/index.pi#LeftSidebar.description and
            spec/ui/components/left-sidebar/index.pi#LeftSidebar.viewBar ("The bar is always
            there"). The right sidebar doesn't show either, even while a task runs, the queue
            holds a prompt, or it was opened by hand. This is an exception to
            spec/ui/components/right-sidebar/index.pi#RightSidebar.showing, and the body's
            right-sidebar button and Ctrl+Alt+B (Cmd+Alt+B on macOS) do nothing while Planning
            is selected.

            This works like the chat input, which already hides while Planning is selected, as
            spec/scope/application/Body.pi#Body.tabs says: both sidebars go at once with nothing
            sliding, and come back at once when Chat, a file or a task tab is selected. They
            come back as they were left: the left sidebar's view, collapsed state, width,
            scroll and selection, and the right sidebar's width and whether it was opened by
            hand. If a task started or ended while Planning was showing, the right sidebar comes
            back in whatever state that leaves it.

            Update spec/scope/application/Body.pi#Body.description and
            spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.description so Planning is placed across the
            full window width.
            \\\
        result:
            \\\
            I've updated the spec so that while Planning's tab is selected, neither sidebar shows and Planning spans the full window width under the ribbon. `piton check` reports no problems, `piton build` wrote all 86 files, and the new text shows up in the rebuilt reference.

            The code should follow these anchors:
            - **`Body.description`** (`.claude/reference/scope/application/Body.md#description`), new paragraph:
              - The whole left sidebar hides, view bar included. The right sidebar hides too, even while a task runs, a prompt is queued, or it was opened by hand.
              - Both sidebars go and come back at once with no slide, just as the chat input does.
              - They come back as they were left: the left sidebar's view, collapsed state, width, scroll and selection, and the right sidebar's width and whether it was opened by hand.
              - If a task started or ended while Planning was showing, the right sidebar comes back in whatever state that leaves.
            - **`Body.tabs`** (`#tabs`): the tab bar runs from the window's left edge to its right while Planning is selected, and the paragraph about Planning hiding the chat input now also says the sidebars hide.
            - **`Planning.description`** (`.claude/reference/scope/planning/index.md#description`): the board fills from the tab bar to the bottom of the window, across its whole width.
            - **`LeftSidebar.description`** and **`LeftSidebar.viewBar`** (`.claude/reference/ui/components/left-sidebar/index.md`): these state the exception. Hidden while Planning is selected is the one time the view bar isn't there.
            - **`RightSidebar.showing`** (`.claude/reference/ui/components/right-sidebar/index.md#showing`): the sidebar isn't there while Planning is selected. Its button in the tab bar and Ctrl+Alt+B (Cmd+Alt+B on macOS) do nothing then, and it keeps its width and whether it was opened by hand.

            The spec leaves that button visible but doing nothing; it doesn't hide it or show it as disabled.
            \\\

    sentFrom: HideBothSidebarsWhilePlanningIsShowing

    model: opus
